Mathematical Abstraction
File size. An encrypted file is bigger than the original, and you can predict exactly how much. For a plain file of \(n\) bytes:
\[
c = 16 \left( \left\lfloor \frac{n}{16} \right\rfloor + 1 \right), \qquad
h = 2\,(64 + 1 + 64 + 1 + 2c), \qquad
\ell = \left\lceil \frac{h}{80} \right\rceil
\]
where:

\(n\) is the size of the plain text in bytes;
\(c\) is the size of the ciphertext in bytes: AES works on 16-byte blocks, and the padding always adds between 1 and 16 bytes;
\(\lfloor x \rfloor\) rounds down and \(\lceil x \rceil\) rounds up;
\(64\) is the 32-byte salt written as hexadecimal, and the second \(64\) is the 32-byte HMAC checksum in hexadecimal; the two \(1\)s are the newlines between the parts, and \(2c\) is the ciphertext in hexadecimal;
the outer factor 2 is there because Ansible hex-encodes that whole text once more;
\(h\) is the number of hexadecimal characters in the file body;
\(\ell\) is the number of lines, because the body is wrapped at 80 characters.

Example, the lab’s secrets/vault.yml. The plain text vault_db_password: lemonade-demo-secret plus a newline is \(n = 40\) bytes.

\(c = 16 \cdot (\lfloor 40/16 \rfloor + 1) = 16 \cdot (2 + 1) = 48\) bytes.
\(h = 2 \cdot (64 + 1 + 64 + 1 + 2 \cdot 48) = 2 \cdot 226 = 452\) characters.
\(\ell = \lceil 452 / 80 \rceil = \lceil 5.65 \rceil = 6\) lines.

Measured on the real file: 6 lines and 452 characters after the header. With the 26-byte header line and 6 newlines, the file is \(26 + 452 + 6 = 484\) bytes, which matches wc -c.
Why the password must be long. An attacker who copies the file can try passwords offline. PBKDF2 makes each guess cost \(k\) HMAC computations:
\[
T = \frac{\lvert \Sigma \rvert^{L} \cdot k}{R}
\]
where:

\(\Sigma\) is the set of characters the password could use, and \(\lvert \Sigma \rvert\) is how many there are;
\(L\) is the password length in characters;
\(\lvert \Sigma \rvert^{L}\) is the number of possible passwords;
\(k = 10\,000\) is the number of PBKDF2 rounds in vault format 1.1;
\(R\) is how many HMAC computations per second the attacker can do;
\(T\) is the worst-case time, in seconds, to try every password.

This is an estimate with an assumed attacker speed. Assume a strong GPU rig with \(R = 10^{10}\) per second.
Example 1, 8 lowercase letters: \(\lvert \Sigma \rvert = 26\) and \(L = 8\). Then \(26^8 \approx 2.09 \times 10^{11}\), so \(T \approx 2.09 \times 10^{11} \cdot 10^{4} / 10^{10} = 2.09 \times 10^{5}\) s, which is about 2.4 days.
Example 2, 16 lowercase letters: \(26^{16} \approx 4.36 \times 10^{22}\), so \(T \approx 4.36 \times 10^{22} \cdot 10^{4} / 10^{10} = 4.36 \times 10^{16}\) s, which is about 1.4 billion years.
Doubling the length squares the number of possible passwords. A long random vault password, kept in a password manager, is what makes the decoder ring safe.

Mathematical Abstraction
Batch sizes. Let the play have \(N\) hosts and serial the list \(s_1, s_2, \dots, s_m\). Each entry becomes a batch size:
\[
b_j =
\begin{cases}
\max\!\left(1, \left\lfloor \dfrac{q_j}{100} \cdot N \right\rfloor\right) & \text{if } s_j \text{ is a percentage } q_j\% \\[1ex]
s_j & \text{if } s_j \text{ is a number}
\end{cases}
\qquad j = 1, 2, \dots
\]
where:

\(N\) is the number of hosts the play targets;
\(s_j\) is the \(j\)-th entry of the serial list, and \(q_j\) is its value when written as a percentage;
\(\lfloor x \rfloor\) rounds down;
\(b_j\) is the size of batch \(j\); after the list ends, the last entry is used again; the final batch takes whatever hosts remain, so it can be smaller.

Example 1, the lab: \(N = 2\), serial: 1. So \(b_1 = 1\), \(b_2 = 1\): two batches, web1 then web2.
Example 2, a summer fleet of \(N = 10\) web servers with serial: [1, "30%", "100%"]:

\(b_1 = 1\) (one canary);
\(b_2 = \max(1, \lfloor 0.30 \cdot 10 \rfloor) = \max(1, 3) = 3\);
\(b_3 = \max(1, \lfloor 1.00 \cdot 10 \rfloor) = 10\), but only \(10 - 1 - 3 = 6\) hosts remain, so the batch has 6.

Batches of 1, 3 and 6. A real run on ten test hosts printed exactly ['w01'], then ['w02', 'w03', 'w04'], then ['w05', …, 'w10'].
When the run stops. After each task, Ansible compares the failed share of the batch with the limit:
\[
\frac{F}{B} &gt; \frac{p}{100} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{stop the whole run}
\]
where:

\(B\) is the size of the current batch;
\(F\) is the number of hosts in that batch that have failed;
\(p\) is max_fail_percentage, from 0 to 100;
the comparison is strictly greater than: hitting the limit exactly is still allowed.

Example 1: \(B = 4\), \(p = 25\), and one host fails. \(F/B = 1/4 = 0.25\), which is not greater than \(0.25\), so the run continues. A real test did exactly that.
Example 2: \(B = 4\), \(p = 25\), two hosts fail. \(2/4 = 0.5 &gt; 0.25\), so the run stops. The real test printed NO MORE HOSTS LEFT and never started the next batch.
Example 3, the lab: \(p = 0\). Any failure gives \(F/B \ge 1/B &gt; 0\), so the first broken server stops the rollout.
Capacity during the rollout. While a batch is drained, the rest of the fleet carries the traffic:
\[
\text{capacity} = \frac{N - b}{N}
\]
where \(N\) is the number of web servers and \(b\) the size of the batch currently drained.
Example. The lab: \(N = 2\) and \(b = 1\), so capacity is \((2 - 1)/2 = 50\%\). If the stand’s peak traffic needs 70% of both servers, a Saturday-noon rollout would overload web2. Roll out in a quiet hour, or add a third server: \((3 - 1)/3 \approx 67\%\). For the summer fleet, the 6-host batch leaves \((10 - 6)/10 = 40\%\), so a large last batch should only be used when traffic is low.

Mathematical Abstraction
For a variable name \(v\) and a host \(h\), several sources may define a value. Ansible keeps the one with the highest rank:
\[
\operatorname{value}(v, h) = x_{s^\ast}, \qquad s^\ast = \arg\max_{s \in D(v,h)} r(s)
\]
where:

\(v\) is a variable name, such as lemonade_nginx_workers;
\(h\) is a host, such as web1;
\(D(v,h)\) is the set of sources that define \(v\) for \(h\) (role defaults, host_vars, -e and so on);
\(r(s)\) is the rank of source \(s\) on the 22-level ladder above, from 1 (lowest) to 22 (highest);
\(\arg\max\) picks the source with the largest rank; \(s^\ast\) is that winning source;
\(x_s\) is the value that source \(s\) gives.

Example 1, web1, normal run. \(D = \lbrace \text{role defaults}, \text{host vars file} \rbrace\) with ranks \(r = 2\) (value 1) and \(r = 9\) (value 4). The largest rank is \(\max(2, 9) = 9\), so the value is 4.
Example 2, web2, normal run. Only role defaults define the variable: \(D = \lbrace \text{role defaults} \rbrace\), so \(s^\ast\) is the defaults with \(r = 2\), and the value is 1.
Example 3, both hosts with -e lemonade_nginx_workers=8. Extra vars join \(D\) with \(r = 22\). Since \(22 &gt; 9\) and \(22 &gt; 2\), both hosts get 8, which matches the diff above.
When the values are dictionaries or lists, “keep the winner” means the winner replaces the others completely. The menu shows this. The role default list has \(1\) drink and group_vars/web.yml has \(3\), so the result has \(3\) drinks, not \(1 + 3 = 4\).

Ad hoc commands are quick, but they disappear when you close the terminal. Setting up a Lemonade web server takes several steps in a fixed order: create folders, write the nginx config, write the menu page, reload nginx. You want those steps:

written down in Git, so a teammate can review them;
run in the same order on every web server;
safe to run again tomorrow.

A playbook is that written-down list.

