Mathematical Abstraction
PLAY RECAP counts tasks per host. The two most important counters are linked:
\[
o_h = c_h + u_h
\]
where:

\(h\) is one host, for example web1;
\(o_h\) is the ok= number for \(h\): every task that finished successfully;
\(c_h\) is the changed= number: successful tasks that modified the host;
\(u_h\) is the number of successful tasks that found everything already correct. It is not printed, but you can compute it.

Example. First run of site.yml on web1: \(o = 4\) and \(c = 3\), so \(u = 4 - 3 = 1\). The one unchanged task is the handler “Reload nginx”, which uses debug and never reports a change. Second run: \(o = 3\), \(c = 0\), so \(u = 3\). All three role tasks found the files already correct.
YAML’s number rules explain why mode must be quoted. A file mode is a number in base 8 (octal):
\[
m = \sum_{i=0}^{k} d_i \cdot 8^{i}
\]
where:

\(m\) is the permission value as an ordinary (base 10) number;
\(d_i\) is the \(i\)-th octal digit, counting from the right and starting at \(i = 0\); each \(d_i \in \lbrace 0, 1, \dots, 7 \rbrace\);
\(k + 1\) is the number of digits.

Example 1. The intended mode 0644, read as octal: \(m = 6 \cdot 8^2 + 4 \cdot 8^1 + 4 \cdot 8^0 = 384 + 32 + 4 = 420\). That is rw-r--r--. YAML reads unquoted 0644 as octal 420 too, and quoted "0644" is passed to Ansible as text and read as octal. Both are fine.
Example 2. Unquoted 644, without the leading zero, is the decimal number 644. Turn it back into octal by dividing by 8 repeatedly:

\(644 = 80 \cdot 8 + 4\), so \(d_0 = 4\);
\(80 = 10 \cdot 8 + 0\), so \(d_1 = 0\);
\(10 = 1 \cdot 8 + 2\), so \(d_2 = 2\);
\(1 = 0 \cdot 8 + 1\), so \(d_3 = 1\).

So decimal 644 is octal 1204: the sticky bit, -w- for the owner, nothing for the group and r-- for everyone else. The owner can no longer read the file. Quoting the mode, "0644", removes the doubt.

Every piece of syntax









Syntax
What it does
Rule / convention
Example




{ expr }
Prints the value of an expression.
Spaces inside the braces are the convention.
{ lemonade_app_port }


{% if %}…{% elif %}…{% else %}…{% endif %}
Includes text only when a condition holds.
Every if needs endif.
{% if lemonade_nginx_workers &gt; 2 %}big{% endif %}


{% for x in list %}…{% endfor %}
Repeats text for each item.
loop.index counts from 1.
{% for drink in lemonade_menu %}


{% set x = … %}
Creates a template-local variable.

{% set total = lemonade_menu \| map(attribute='price') \| sum %}


{# … #}
A comment that never reaches the output.

{# keep in sync with app #}


{%- / -%}
Strips whitespace before or after the tag.
Use when output has unwanted blank lines.
{%- for d in menu %}


x \| filter(args)
Applies a filter. Chain them left to right.

{ menu \| map(attribute='name') \| join(', ') }


x is test
A yes/no test.
is not negates.
{ port is defined }


~
Joins values as text.
Safer than + for mixed types.
{ 'web' ~ 1 } → web1


in
Membership.

{ 'web1' in groups['web'] }

Worked example
The lab’s nginx.conf.j2:
# {{ ansible_managed }}
worker_processes {{ lemonade_nginx_workers }};
server {
    listen 80;
    location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:{{ lemonade_app_port }}; }
}
And index.html.j2, with a loop:
&lt;h1&gt;Sunny Lemonade Stand&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
{% for drink in lemonade_menu %}
  &lt;li&gt;{{ drink.name }}: ${{ '%.2f' | format(drink.price) }}&lt;/li&gt;
{% endfor %}
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Served by {{ inventory_hostname }}, version {{ lemonade_version }}&lt;/p&gt;
After ansible-playbook site.yml, web1 has:
# Ansible managed
worker_processes 4;
server {
    listen 80;
    location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080; }
}
&lt;h1&gt;Sunny Lemonade Stand&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Classic Lemonade: $2.50&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Pink Lemonade: $3.00&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Iced Tea: $2.00&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Served by web1, version 1.0.0&lt;/p&gt;
There are no blank lines where the {% for %} and {% endfor %} tags were, because trim_blocks is on by default.
